   "text": {
    "L1": "A bar graph uses bars to show amounts. First, read its title and labels.",
    "L2": "Before reading a bar, check the scale. Each line may stand for five, ten, or one hundred.",
    "L3": "The scale must start at zero and rise in equal steps. For values from 12 to 30, steps of five fit well.",
    "L4": "Do not just count grid lines. Follow the bar top to the scale and multiply the number of steps by what each line stands for.",
    "L5": "Here the scale is 0, 5, 10, and so on to 35. Monday is 20, Tuesday is 35, and Wednesday is 15.",
    "L6": "To compare two bars, read both values from the scale and subtract. For 80 and 45, the difference is 35.",
    "L7": "A scale that skips zero or uses uneven steps can make a small difference look huge. That graph misleads us.",
    "L8": "Now check yourself. Each line stands for 10 children, and the mango bar reaches the fourth line."
   },
